/**
@page modifying Modifying fields and checksums

Fields are written through setters, the write half of accessor-mediated access
(reading is in @ref reading). Values are passed in the byte order the getters
use; see @ref reading.

@section modifying_set Setters

@c set_<field> writes the field and, where the field has a checksum, updates it
incrementally. This is the standard way to modify a packet:

@code{.c}
bnt_ipv4__set_ttl(&ip, 64);
@endcode

When a field also feeds the next layer's checksum, the incremental update
follows through for the headers BONet supports. Rewriting an IPv4 address fixes
both the IPv4 header checksum and the TCP or UDP checksum that covers it through
the pseudo-header in one call:

@code{.c}
SEC("xdp")
int dnat(struct xdp_md *ctx)
{
    bnt_eth  eth;
    bnt_ipv4 ip;

    if (bnt_eth__parse(ctx, 0, &eth))  return XDP_PASS;
    if (bnt_eth__next_ipv4(&eth, &ip)) return XDP_PASS;

    /* Redirect to a new destination; both checksums stay correct. */
    if (bnt_ipv4__set_daddr(&ip, bpf_htonl(0x0A000002)))
        return XDP_DROP;

    return XDP_PASS;
}
@endcode

When the upper layer is not a supported L4 header, only the IPv4 header checksum
is updated.

@section modifying_recompute Full recompute

Incremental updates cover almost every case. A full recompute is heavier and is
reserved for changing many fields at once. Write the fields with the @c _no_csum
setters, then rebuild the checksum once:

@code{.c}
bnt_tcp__set_dport_no_csum(&tcp, 8080);
bnt_tcp__set_sport_no_csum(&tcp, 40000);
bnt_tcp__recompute_csum(&tcp, &ip);
@endcode

A recompute is provided only where a header can be rebuilt from scratch, and not
on TC egress, where the kernel owns the checksum.
*/
